a2fe9be9ac7d2bc9981763fc0","filename":"unsolved_math_prioritization/attempts/2303016/READINESS.json","status":"added","additions":13,"deletions":0,"changes":13,"blob_url":"https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/blob/f4039c9c093b10e651ee7fd2e6379073b84238c7/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2303016%2FREADINESS.json","raw_url":"https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/raw/f4039c9c093b10e651ee7fd2e6379073b84238c7/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2303016%2FREADINESS.json","contents_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/AlecKriebel/Math/contents/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2303016%2FREADINESS.json?ref=f4039c9c093b10e651ee7fd2e6379073b84238c7","patch":"@@ -0,0 +1,13 @@\n+{\n+  \"review_hash\": \"d483c473c60d1dad134cc52b6eadfb1abac45bf24452902f79e17bdfaaab910c\",\n+  \"exact_claim\": \"For compact E in R^n,n>=3, give a direct proof that the points x in E where integral_0^1 cap(E intersect closed B(x,r))/r^(n-1) dr is finite form a polar set; perhaps demonstrate sharpness. Source2018 printed65/PDF66 Problem3.16, formula3.7; Update3.16 reports no progress.\",\n+  \"primary_sources\": [\n+    \"https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200\",\n+    \"https://www.numdam.org/item/10.5802/aif.944.pdf\"\n+  ],\n+  \"literature_checked_at\": \"2026-10-02T16:16:00Z\",\n+  \"success_test\": \"A direct capacity/energy argument without importing Kellogg or Dirichlet irregular-point results, with all measurability and capacity normalizations explicit. Natural sharpness claims must be precisely formulated rather than an undefined universal optimality assertion.\",\n+  \"budget\": \"Five genuine author turns unless a complete source-requested candidate earlier, then independent full review.\",\n+  \"prior_attempt_gap\": \"No exact ID/code, Kellogg, Rippon+thin PR match, nor recovered object-path/commit/recovery-inventory match. Imported report is only statement reading and web search, not proof work.\",\n+  \"duplicate_check\": \"Live branch scan and379 recovered refs; exact-ID/code/method aliases searched. A blank branch would not alone be an attempt. Inaccessible deleted objects not certified absent.\"\n+}"},{"sha":"e0e91032a86ad7665aee856dcb9b2256d56a7213","filename":"unsolved_math_prioritization/attempts/2303016/README.md","status":"added","additions":30,"deletions":0,"changes":30,"blob_url":"https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/blob/f4039c9c093b10e651ee7fd2e6379073b84238c7/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2303016%2FREADME.md","raw_url":"https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/raw/f4039c9c093b10e651ee7fd2e6379073b84238c7/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2303016%2FREADME.md","contents_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/AlecKriebel/Math/contents/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2303016%2FREADME.md?ref=f4039c9c093b10e651ee7fd2e6379073b84238c7","patch":"@@ -0,0 +1,30 @@\n+# Problem 2303016: credited direct capacity proof\n+\n+**Reviewed disposition: already_solved, 1/5. Full source/method/proof review: PASS.**\n+\n+For compact E in R^n, n >= 3, the points of E where the integrated local-capacity Wiener expression is finite form a polar set. The source already knows this consequence of Kellogg and requests a direct proof. This packet supplies a method-compliant Newtonian energy argument, crediting the classical capacitary strategy of Hedberg–Wolff (1983), pp. 173–174. No novelty or priority claim is made.\n+\n+- [Complete direct proof and precise sharpness](TURN_1.md)\n+- [Exact original scope and method requirement](SOURCE_NORMALIZATION.md)\n+- [Independent full source/method review](final_review/REVIEW.md)\n+- [Result and limitations](RESULT.md)\n+\n+The argument proves its equilibrium first-variation and weak potential bounds, localizes to uniformly small integral tails, and obtains an energy contradiction. It does not invoke Kellogg, a Dirichlet-boundary theorem or the Wiener equivalence. Standard Borel capacitability and capacity-zero/polar equivalence remain explicit foundations. Ordinary Choquet capacitability is distinguished from the fine-topological Choquet property.\n+\n+The denominator exponent is critical under every positive power weakening, and an uncountable polar set can be the entire exceptional set. No universal claim about all nonintegrable gauge weakenings is made.\n+\n+All 14 frozen author files and all review files are unchanged; their historical pending-review wording is retained, while this wrapper records the completed PASS.\n+\n+## Replay\n+\n+Author checks use standard-library Python:\n+\n+    python verify_turn1.py > /tmp/turn1.json\n+    cmp TURN_1_CHECKS.json /tmp/turn1.json\n+\n+Independent replay is already portable when given this directory. It requires **SymPy** in addition to Python's standard library:\n+\n+    python final_review/check.py . > /tmp/review.json\n+    cmp final_review/CHECKS.json /tmp/review.json\n+\n+The author has 2,690 scalar controls; the independent review has 1,909 controls and replays the author output. The analytic all-set proof was reviewed separately; finite controls do not certify polarity. Primary PDF identities are recorded in SOURCE_MANIFEST.json, but PDFs/imported records/private material are not redistributed."},{"sha":"d291b63640c2893325882796de501ada31a2b848","filename":"unsolved_math_prioritization/attempts/2303016/RESEARCH_LOG.md","status":"added","additions":7,"deletions":0,"changes":7,"blob_url":"https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/blob/f4039c9c093b10e651ee7fd2e6379073b84238c7/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2303016%2FRESEARCH_LOG.md","raw_url":"https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/raw/f4039c9c093b10e651ee7fd2e6379073b84238c7/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2303016%2FRESEARCH_LOG.md","contents_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/AlecKriebel/Math/contents/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2303016%2FRESEARCH_LOG.md?ref=f4039c9c093b10e651ee7fd2e6379073b84238c7","patch":"@@ -0,0 +1,7 @@\n+# One-turn research log\n+\n+The exact original and its update were recovered and visually checked. The imported report was only triage, and no accessible prior substantive attempt appeared under the target or method aliases. Primary Hedberg–Wolff literature supplied a direct capacitary strategy.\n+\n+Turn1 reconstructed that argument directly for Newtonian energy, including compact-continuity/measurability, equilibrium first variation, a nearest-support weak potential bound, localization to a small positive-capacity subset, and the layer-cake contradiction. It also supplied critical-power and uncountable-polar-example sharpness. All estimates are symbolic; the companion finite arithmetic checks are only controls.\n+\n+The complete method candidate is frozen after one substantive turn. No new theorem, novelty, or full all-gauge optimality is claimed. Four unused turns are not consumed after a full candidate; independent review is required before disposition/publication."},{"sha":"8c3910ecbdc67e625185fc826208e519c638aa68","filename":"unsolved_math_prioritization/attempts/2303016/RESULT.md","status":"added","additions":11,"deletions":0,"changes":11,"blob_url":"https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/blob/f4039c9c093b10e651ee7fd2e6379073b84238c7/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2303016%2FRESULT.md","raw_url":"https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/raw/f4039c9c093b10e651ee7fd2e6379073b84238c7/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2303016%2FRESULT.md","contents_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/AlecKriebel/Math/contents/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2303016%2FRESULT.md?ref=f4039c9c093b10e651ee7fd2e6379073b84238c7","patch":"@@ -0,0 +1,11 @@\n+# Complete one-turn direct-proof candidate\n+\n+Problem2303016 / AMR-022-3016. The exact requested direct proof is supplied in TURN_1.md. Recommended disposition after independent full review: **already_solved, 1/5**, as a credited reconstruction of classical capacitary reasoning, with no novelty claim.\n+\n+For every compact E in R^n, n>=3, the points of E with finite integrated Newtonian Wiener capacity expression form a polar set. The proof uses energy minimizers, a proved global weak bound on their potentials, uniform small-tail localization and layer cake. It does not invoke Kellogg or a boundary-regularity theorem.\n+\n+The standard capacity-theoretic foundations retained are Borel capacitability and the equivalence of capacity zero with polarity. The packet distinguishes these from the fine-topological Choquet property; neither requested conclusion nor Wiener equivalence is used as an input.\n+\n+Two explicit sharpness statements accompany the proof: the denominator exponent cannot be decreased by any positive amount, and the exceptional set cannot in general be required to be countable. The source's optional “best possible” language is not interpreted as an unformulated all-gauge theorem.\n+\n+Credit goes to the known Kellogg conclusion and the direct capacitary-measure strategy of Hedberg–Wolff(1983), pp.173–174. The2018 source still reports no progress to its editors; this packet provides an explicit method-compliant specialization, not a global priority claim. Author search stops at this complete first-turn candidate. Independent full review is pending."},{"sha":"94b7e5fb69dbe70e0c469d72caeed78f889bed16","filename":"unsolved_math_prioritization/attempts/2303016/SOURCE_MANIFEST.json","status":"added","additions":24,"deletions":0,"changes":24,"blob_url":"https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/blob/f4039c9c093b10e651ee7fd2e6379073b84238c7/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2303016%2FSOURCE_MANIFEST.json","raw_url":"https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/raw/f4039c9c093b10e651ee7fd2e6379073b84238c7/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2303016%2FSOURCE_MANIFEST.json","contents_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/AlecKriebel/Math/contents/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2303016%2FSOURCE_MANIFEST.json?ref=f4039c9c093b10e651ee7fd2e6379073b84238c7","patch":"@@ -0,0 +1,24 @@\n+{\n+  \"primary_pdfs\": [\n+    {\n+      \"file\": \"hayman_lingham_2018.pdf\",\n+      \"bytes\": 1706228,\n+      \"sha256\": \"8e28fd4403a07e4e19a9816b7efaafddf9f475d59cf8c34a8255cb03833ed4f0\",\n+      \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \"file\": \"hedberg_wolff_1983.pdf\",\n+      \"bytes\": 1782353,\n+      \"sha256\": \"f351a967ae723f590d85da9a886ca4f6d8a1a21f7de8315f83180d9dcf3b5006\",\n+      \"url\": \"https://www.numdam.org/item/10.5802/aif.944.pdf\"\n+    }\n+  ],\n+  \"dataset_revision\": \"37e53eabe540fb458758e198be61634bd02ee008\",\n+  \"source_pages\": \"Hayman printed65/PDF66 Problem3.16 and Update; Hedberg-Wolff Theorem2, direct Borel proof pp173-174\",\n+  \"capacity_normalization\": \"kernel |x-y|^(2-n), reciprocal minimum probability energy; source finiteness unchanged by positive dimensional scaling\",\n+  \"unreproved_foundations\": [\n+    \"Borel Choquet capacitability (not the fine-topological Choquet property)\",\n+    \"Borel capacity-zero/polar equivalence\",\n+    \"routine measure compactness and regularity\"\n+  ]\n+}"},{"sha":"01f2ee79a7b9cd421b129585605e92ac777a92b9","filename":"unsolved_math_prioritization/attempts/2303016/SOURCE_NORMALIZATION.md","status":"added","additions":13,"deletions":0,"changes":13,"blob_url":"https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/blob/f4039c9c093b10e651ee7fd2e6379073b84238c7/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2303016%2FSOURCE_NORMALIZATION.md","raw_url":"https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/raw/f4039c9c093b10e651ee7fd2e6379073b84238c7/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2303016%2FSOURCE_NORMALIZATION.md","contents_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/AlecKriebel/Math/contents/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2303016%2FSOURCE_NORMALIZATION.md?ref=f4039c9c093b10e651ee7fd2e6379073b84238c7","patch":"@@ -0,0 +1,13 @@\n+# Exact method request and source scope\n+\n+Problem2303016 / AMR-022-3016 is Hayman–Lingham Problem3.16, credited to P. J. Rippon, printed p.65/PDF p.66 of the2018 source. The equation is numbered(3.7), whereas the catalog retains an unresolved source-label token. The formula itself is unchanged:\n+\n+    integral_0^1 cap(E intersect closed B(x,r)) / r^(n−1) dr < infinity.\n+\n+E is compact in R^n, n>=3. The conclusion concerns only points x **in E**. The theorem that this set is polar is already known via Kellogg; the requested contribution is a **direct proof**, with possible sharpness information. Merely citing Kellogg would not answer the methodological request. Update3.16 reports no progress to the editors.\n+\n+The proposed proof uses Newtonian energy capacity and measure arguments. It does not invoke Kellogg, Dirichlet irregular-point results, or the equivalence of the displayed criterion with potential-theoretic thinness. The name “thin point” is only shorthand for the integral-finiteness set in this packet.\n+\n+The precise sharpness results proved are: every positive decrease in the denominator exponent makes the condition finite everywhere on every compact set; every integrable multiplicative weight behaves similarly; and the exceptional set can be an uncountable compact polar set. No assertion is made about arbitrary nonintegrable gauge weakenings.\n+\n+Hedberg–Wolff, *Thin sets in nonlinear potential theory*, Ann. Inst. Fourier33(4)(1983),161–187, Theorem2 and its direct Borel-set proof on pp.173–174, use a small-Wiener-integral compact subset and its capacitary measure. The current argument credits and specializes that strategy to Newtonian energy while proving the needed weak potential bound explicitly. It does not invoke their nonlinear Theorem1, silently identify Bessel and Newtonian capacities, or claim a new general Kellogg theorem."},{"sha":"7307e247f1dc6783c4d29e24156e6e513c699392","filename":"unsolved_math_prioritization/attempts/2303016/TURN_1.md","status":"added","additions":171,"deletions":0,"changes":171,"blob_url":"https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/blob/f4039c9c093b10e651ee7fd2e6379073b84238c7/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2303016%2FTURN_1.md","raw_url":"https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/raw/f4039c9c093b10e651ee7fd2e6379073b84238c7/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2303016%2FTURN_1.md","contents_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/AlecKriebel/Math/contents/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2303016%2FTURN_1.md?ref=f4039c9c093b10e651ee7fd2e6379073b84238c7","patch":"@@ -0,0 +1,171 @@\n+# Turn 1: a direct energy proof of the thin-point conclusion\n+\n+Problem2303016 / AMR-022-3016, Hayman–Lingham Problem3.16. Complete candidate for the requested direct proof, with two precise sharpness statements. The method is a Newtonian specialization/reconstruction of the capacitary strategy in Hedberg–Wolff, *Thin sets in nonlinear potential theory* (1983), pp.173–174. No novelty or priority claim is made.\n+\n+## 1. Normalization and foundational inputs\n+\n+Put s=n−2>0 and use the Newtonian kernel k(x,y)=|x−y|^(−s), with infinite diagonal value. For a compact set K define\n+\n+    cap(K) = 1 / inf{ I(mu) : mu is a probability measure supported on K },\n+    I(mu) = double_integral k(x,y) dmu(x)dmu(y),\n+\n+with1/infinity=0 and cap(empty)=0. Changing this capacity by a fixed positive dimensional factor does not affect the question's finiteness condition or polar conclusion.\n+\n+We use the standard foundational facts that Newtonian capacity is a Choquet capacity on Borel sets, and that a Borel set has capacity zero exactly when it is polar. Thus a nonpolar Borel set contains a compact subset carrying a finite-energy probability measure. This is the measure-theoretic Choquet capacitability theorem, not the fine-topological Choquet property that itself implies Kellogg. These general capacity/measure facts are explicitly retained as background; they are not Kellogg's theorem, the Wiener regularity criterion, or a Dirichlet-boundary result. The arguments below prove all special equilibrium-potential estimates they use.\n+\n+Weak compactness of probabilities on a compact set and lower semicontinuity of the nonnegative kernel give an energy-minimizing probability mu when0<cap(K)<infinity. Write J=I(mu). If D=diam(K), then\n+\n+    J >= D^(−s) > 0,                                (1.1)\n+\n+since distances between points of K are at most D. Positive capacity excludes D=0. Compact sets have finite capacity by this same bound.\n+\n+The potential is U_mu(x)=integral |x−y|^(−s) dmu(y).\n+\n+## 2. The equilibrium property needed here, without a q.e. theorem\n+\n+For any Borel A with mu(A)>0, the probability nu=mu|_A/mu(A) has finite energy. Minimality under the variation(1−t)mu+t nu gives\n+\n+    integral U_mu dnu >= J.\n+\n+All cross energies in this variation are finite because nu is bounded by a constant multiple of mu. Hence\n+\n+    integral_A U_mu dmu >= J mu(A)\n+\n+for every such A. It follows that U_mu>=J mu-almost everywhere. Since integral U_mu dmu=J, in fact\n+\n+    U_mu=J mu-almost everywhere.                     (2.1)\n+\n+No assertion about exceptional boundary points has been used.\n+\n+Let S=supp(mu) and G={x in S:U_mu(x)=J}. Then G is dense in S: every relatively open nonempty portion of the support has positive mu-mass. The potential is lower semicontinuous by Fatou's lemma. Approximating any x in S by points of G therefore gives\n+\n+    U_mu(x)<=J, x in S.                              (2.2)\n+\n+## 3. A global weak maximum bound from nearest support points\n+\n+Set C=2^s. We claim\n+\n+    U_mu(x)<=C J for every x in R^n.                 (3.1)\n+\n+Section2 handles x in S. If x lies outside S, let d=dist(x,S)>0 and choose a nearest point y in S. For epsilon>0 choose y_epsilon in G with |y_epsilon−y|<epsilon. For every z in S,\n+\n+    |y_epsilon−z| <= |y_epsilon−x|+|x−z|\n+       <= d+epsilon+|x−z|\n+       <= (2+epsilon/d)|x−z|.\n+\n+After raising to the negative s power and integrating,\n+\n+    U_mu(x) <= (2+epsilon/d)^s U_mu(y_epsilon)\n+             = (2+epsilon/d)^s J.\n+\n+Let epsilon decrease to0. This proves(3.1). It is a deliberately non-sharp bound and does not import a potential maximum principle, Frostman's q.e. statement, or Kellogg's theorem.\n+\n+For a compact A contained in K, put m=mu(A). If m>0, the restricted measure satisfies\n+\n+    I(mu|_A) <= C J m.\n+\n+Its normalized probability is admissible in the energy definition of cap(A), so\n+\n+    cap(A) >= m²/I(mu|_A) >= m/(C J).\n+\n+For m=0 the same final inequality is immediate. Thus\n+\n+    mu(A) <= C J cap(A)                              (3.2)\n+\n+for every compact A contained in K.\n+\n+## 4. Measurability of the exceptional set\n+\n+For the given compact E, put\n+\n+    c(x,r)=cap(E intersect closed B(x,r)), r>0,\n+    W_E(x)=integral_0^1 c(x,r) r^(−s−1) dr,\n+    T={x in E:W_E(x)<infinity}.\n+\n+The map(x,r)↦c(x,r) is upper semicontinuous. If(x_j,r_j)→(x,r), then for every epsilon>0 and sufficiently large j,\n+\n+    E intersect closed B(x_j,r_j)\n+       is contained in E intersect closed B(x,r+epsilon).\n+\n+Capacity is continuous along decreasing compact sets, so taking limsup and then epsilon→0 gives the assertion. For clarity, compact continuity follows directly from the energy definition: choose minimizing probabilities on a decreasing sequence of compact sets; a weakly convergent subsequence is supported on their intersection, and lower semicontinuity of energy yields the reverse inequality to monotonicity. The case when the limiting capacities are zero is immediate.\n+\n+Therefore c is Borel measurable, and nonnegative parameter integration makes W_E and every truncated/tail integral Borel. Thus T is Borel and the Borel-capacitability input from Section1 applies.\n+\n+## 5. Localize a hypothetical positive-capacity exceptional set\n+\n+Suppose, for contradiction, that T is nonpolar. Take a compact K_0 contained in T with positive capacity and a finite-energy probability rho on K_0. At every x in K_0,\n+\n+    integral_0^delta c(x,r) r^(−s−1) dr →0 as delta→0.\n+\n+Let epsilon=1/(4Cs). The increasing Borel sets\n+\n+    T_j={x in K_0: integral_0^(1/j) c(x,r) r^(−s−1) dr <= epsilon}\n+\n+cover K_0. Some T_j has positive rho-mass. Fix such a j and delta=1/j. Cover R^n by countably many small closed cubes of diameter at most delta/4. Some T_j intersected with a cube has positive rho-mass. Inner regularity of rho supplies a compact subset K of that intersection with rho(K)>0.\n+\n+The normalized restriction of rho to K still has finite energy, so cap(K)>0. Moreover,\n+\n+    diam(K)<=delta/4,\n+    integral_0^delta c(x,r) r^(−s−1) dr <=1/(4Cs)\n+          for every x in K.                         (5.1)\n+\n+This uniform small-tail localization is the essential step. It is justified by measurable exhaustion and inner regularity, not an unproved uniformity assertion on all thin points.\n+\n+## 6. The contradiction by layer cake\n+\n+Let mu be the energy-minimizing probability on K and J=I(mu). Tonelli's theorem and the elementary identity\n+\n+    t^(−s)=s integral_t^infinity r^(−s−1) dr\n+\n+give, for every x,\n+\n+    U_mu(x)=s integral_0^infinity mu(closed B(x,r)) r^(−s−1) dr.  (6.1)\n+\n+Open versus closed balls does not change this radial integral; the same identity follows pointwise before integrating in y, including the infinite diagonal case.\n+\n+For x in K and r<=delta, apply(3.2) to K intersect closed B(x,r), and then monotonicity with K contained in E:\n+\n+    mu(closed B(x,r)) <= C J c(x,r).\n+\n+For r>delta use only total mass1. Equations(5.1) and(6.1) imply\n+\n+    U_mu(x) <= C J s integral_0^delta c(x,r) r^(−s−1) dr + delta^(−s)\n+             <= J/4 + delta^(−s).\n+\n+By(1.1) and diam(K)<=delta/4,\n+\n+    delta^(−s) <= J (diam(K)/delta)^s <= J/4^s <= J/4.\n+\n+Thus U_mu(x)<=J/2 for every x in K. This contradicts U_mu=J mu-almost everywhere and J>0. Therefore cap(T)=0, and T is polar.\n+\n+This proves the exact original integral-finiteness conclusion directly from capacity energy, with no appeal to harmonic boundary regularity or the Wiener equivalence.\n+\n+## 7. Precise critical-power sharpness\n+\n+For every x,r, the diameter bound gives\n+\n+    c(x,r)<=cap(closed B(x,r))<=2^s r^s.\n+\n+If the denominator power n−1=s+1 is weakened to n−1−eta for any eta>0, then\n+\n+    integral_0^1 c(x,r) r^(−s−1+eta) dr <= 2^s/eta\n+\n+at **every** point of every compact E. Taking E to be a ball gives a positive-capacity exceptional set. A ball has positive capacity because normalized volume measure has finite Newtonian energy: the kernel exponent s=n−2 is strictly below the ambient dimension n and its local radial integral is proportional to integral_0^2 r dr.\n+\n+Thus the denominator exponent is critical. More generally any nonnegative weight a(r) with integral_0^1 a(r) dr/r finite makes the weighted criterion finite everywhere by the same bound, so no polar conclusion survives for that class of weakenings. This does not assert that every smaller, nonintegrable weight fails; the source's optional “best possible” clause is not promoted to an unformulated all-gauge theorem.\n+\n+## 8. Sharpness of the exceptional-set size\n+\n+Every compact polar set P has cap(P intersect closed B)=0, so all its points satisfy the criterion. The exceptional set therefore need not be empty, finite or countable.\n+\n+For an explicit uncountable example take a line segment of length1 in R^n, n>=3. Every probability rho on that segment has infinite energy. Indeed, for d=|x−y|<=1,\n+\n+    d^(−s)>=d^(−1)>=sum_{k>=0} 2^(k−1) 1_{d<=2^(−k)}.\n+\n+Partition the segment into2^k intervals. The rho-product mass of same-interval pairs is the sum of squared interval masses, at least2^(−k). Integrating the displayed kernel bound yields a contribution at least1/2 for every k, hence infinite energy. Atoms simply give infinite diagonal energy as well. Thus the segment has zero capacity and is polar, while every one of its uncountably many points satisfies W_E=0.\n+\n+## 9. Credit and audit limits\n+\n+Hedberg–Wolff's general proof for Borel sets uses a small-capacity-integral compact subset and its capacitary measure to obtain an energy contradiction. This packet gives the explicit Newtonian version, with a proved non-sharp potential bound and measured localization. Its elementary sharpness examples are not novelty claims.\n+\n+The only unreproved capacity-theoretic foundations are Borel capacitability and the equivalence of Newtonian capacity zero with polarity, together with routine measure compactness/regularity. These are not the requested conclusion in disguise and do not use Kellogg or the Wiener criterion. Independent review must verify that separation as well as every estimate above. The source-method candidate is frozen after this one substantive turn; no further author search before review."},{"sha":"1423052014ad810f66102bfd895fe05d203c842d","filename":"unsolved_math_prioritization/attempts/2303016/TURN_1_CHECKS.json","status":"added","additions":5,"deletions":0,"changes":5,"blob_url":"https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/blob/f4039c9c093b10e651ee7fd2e6379073b84238c7/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2303016%2FTURN_1_CHECKS.json","raw_url":"https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/raw/f4039c9c093b10e651ee7fd2e6379073b84238c7/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2303016%2FTURN_1_CHECKS.json","contents_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/AlecKriebel/Math/contents/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2303016%2FTURN_1_CHECKS.json?ref=f4039c9c093b10e651ee7fd2e6379073b84238c7","patch":"@@ -0,0 +1,5 @@\n+{\n+  \"assertions\": 2690,\n+  \"method\": \"No finite computation certifies polarity or capacitability.\",\n+  \"scope\": \"Exact constants, distance inequalities, first-variation algebra and dyadic sharpness sanity checks; the all-set proof is TURN_1.md.\"\n+}"},{"sha":"4674b59fa067ee0c884a48d0ec78a3447eb59f3b","filename":"unsolved_math_prioritization/attempts/2303016/TURN_1_LEDGER.jsonl","status":"added","additions":2,"deletions":0,"changes":2,"blob_url":"https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/blob/f4039c9c093b10e651ee7fd2e6379073b84238c7/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2303016%2FTURN_1_LEDGER.jsonl","raw_url":"https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/raw/f4039c9c093b10e651ee7fd2e6379073b84238c7/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2303016%2FTURN_1_LEDGER.jsonl","contents_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/AlecKriebel/Math/contents/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2303016%2FTURN_1_LEDGER.jsonl?ref=f4039c9c093b10e651ee7fd2e6379073b84238c7","patch":"@@ -0,0 +1,2 @@\n+{\"at\": \"2026-10-02T16:16:08+00:00\", \"id\": \"2303016\", \"status\": \"in_progress\", \"note\": \"Exact original method request and primary direct-capacity literature read; prior-attempt gate clear. Begin one substantive proof turn.\", \"evidence\": {\"review_hash\": \"d483c473c60d1dad134cc52b6eadfb1abac45bf24452902f79e17bdfaaab910c\", \"exact_claim\": \"For compact E in R^n,n>=3, give a direct proof that the points x in E where integral_0^1 cap(E intersect closed B(x,r))/r^(n-1) dr is finite form a polar set; perhaps demonstrate sharpness. Source2018 printed65/PDF66 Problem3.16, formula3.7; Update3.16 reports no progress.\", \"primary_sources\": [\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200\", \"https://www.numdam.org/item/10.5802/aif.944.pdf\"], \"literature_checked_at\": \"2026-10-02T16:16:00Z\", \"success_test\": \"A direct capacity/energy argument without importing Kellogg or Dirichlet irregular-point results, with all measurability and capacity normalizations explicit. Natural sharpness claims must be precisely formulated rather than an undefined universal optimality assertion.\", \"budget\": \"Five genuine author turns unless a complete source-requested candidate earlier, then independent full review.\", \"prior_attempt_gap\": \"No exact ID/code, Kellogg, Rippon+thin PR match, nor recovered object-path/commit/recovery-inventory match. Imported report is only statement reading and web search, not proof work.\", \"duplicate_check\": \"Live branch scan and379 recovered refs; exact-ID/code/method aliases searched. A blank branch would not alone be an attempt. Inaccessible deleted objects not certified absent.\"}, \"statement_hash\": \"72ddb8fb5b205e0232577f513c2371d76b4ce02dfaf6b6358f964a583920f040\", \"review_hash\": \"d483c473c60d1dad134cc52b6eadfb1abac45bf24452902f79e17bdfaaab910c\", \"turns_used\": 0, \"readiness_review_hash\": \"d483c473c60d1dad134cc52b6eadfb1abac45bf24452902f79e17bdfaaab910c\"}\n+{\"at\": \"2026-10-02T16:23:15+00:00\", \"id\": \"2303016\", \"status\": \"candidate_result\", \"note\": \"Turn1 complete direct Newtonian energy proof of the source thin-point conclusion, credited to classical capacitary strategy; explicit weak potential bound and Borel localization, critical-power and uncountable-polar-set sharpness. No Kellogg or Wiener regularity invocation. Full candidate frozen for independent review.\", \"evidence\": {\"review_hash\": \"d483c473c60d1dad134cc52b6eadfb1abac45bf24452902f79e17bdfaaab910c\", \"exact_claim\": \"For compact E in R^n,n>=3, give a direct proof that the points x in E where integral_0^1 cap(E intersect closed B(x,r))/r^(n-1) dr is finite form a polar set; perhaps demonstrate sharpness. Source2018 printed65/PDF66 Problem3.16, formula3.7; Update3.16 reports no progress.\", \"primary_sources\": [\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200\", \"https://www.numdam.org/item/10.5802/aif.944.pdf\"], \"literature_checked_at\": \"2026-10-02T16:16:00Z\", \"success_test\": \"A direct capacity/energy argument without importing Kellogg or Dirichlet irregular-point results, with all measurability and capacity normalizations explicit. Natural sharpness claims must be precisely formulated rather than an undefined universal optimality assertion.\", \"budget\": \"Five genuine author turns unless a complete source-requested candidate earlier, then independent full review.\", \"prior_attempt_gap\": \"No exact ID/code, Kellogg, Rippon+thin PR match, nor recovered object-path/commit/recovery-inventory match. Imported report is only statement reading and web search, not proof work.\", \"duplicate_check\": \"Live branch scan and379 recovered refs; exact-ID/code/method aliases searched. A blank branch would not alone be an attempt. Inaccessible deleted objects not certified absent.\"}, \"statement_hash\": \"72ddb8fb5b205e0232577f513c2371d76b4ce02dfaf6b6358f964a583920f040\", \"review_hash\": \"d483c473c60d1dad134cc52b6eadfb1abac45bf24452902f79e17bdfaaab910c\", \"turns_used\": 1, \"readiness_review_hash\": \"d483c473c60d1dad134cc52b6eadfb1abac45bf24452902f79e17bdfaaab910c\", \"event\": \"proof_attempt_turn\", \"candidate_turn\": 1}"},{"sha":"dd620300af4494ce2f1c4500070b441acacaa49f","filename":"unsolved_math_prioritization/attempts/2303016/TURN_1_MANIFEST.json","status":"added","additions":67,"deletions":0,"changes":67,"blob_url":"https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/blob/f4039c9c093b10e651ee7fd2e6379073b84238c7/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2303016%2FTURN_1_MANIFEST.json","raw_url":"https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/raw/f4039c9c093b10e651ee7fd2e6379073b84238c7/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2303016%2FTURN_1_MANIFEST.json","contents_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/AlecKriebel/Math/contents/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2303016%2FTURN_1_MANIFEST.json?ref=f4039c9c093b10e651ee7fd2e6379073b84238c7","patch":"@@ -0,0 +1,67 @@\n+{\n+  \"id\": 2303016,\n+  \"turns_used\": 1,\n+  \"status\": \"full direct-proof candidate pending review\",\n+  \"files\": [\n+    {\n+      \"path\": \"FINAL_REVIEW_REQUEST.md\",\n+      \"bytes\": 2041,\n+      \"sha256\": \"cf43a35ce30ccfcd50394970fe6e9496d7b402a4bdb63a694beae413496f8f65\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \"path\": \"PRIOR_GATE.json\",\n+      \"bytes\": 616,\n+      \"sha256\": \"4badb1b47a6fe4199c03c934d720e77d4616a6b5d73415a5746442c5fa0dd913\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \"path\": \"READINESS.json\",\n+      \"bytes\": 1379,\n+      \"sha256\": \"4779b3cf3718b19e4ea175fc702614cae89b35deefe1cd2cf8742e7cb0f9b136\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \"path\": \"RESEARCH_LOG.md\",\n+      \"bytes\": 975,\n+      \"sha256\": \"331dd1eb932f2a6c8d4c8daf882f73995265ee00b8adbd5e6babf80a0ac498f9\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \"path\": \"RESULT.md\",\n+      \"bytes\": 1579,\n+      \"sha256\": \"0f79f68154b76497980120948252d094a7e94f3491cff9b5994015c602acfd98\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \"path\": \"SOURCE_MANIFEST.json\",\n+      \"bytes\": 1007,\n+      \"sha256\": \"e0f05a9e9fe63833d4dc5254065e9dffd232ffd496583dd033b88ef28f72b011\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \"path\": \"SOURCE_NORMALIZATION.md\",\n+      \"bytes\": 1875,\n+      \"sha256\": \"48a3b32d465ded74b254e32acaef960bc63034fa51471763b31639096833cc53\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \"path\": \"TURN_1.md\",\n+      \"bytes\": 10140,\n+      \"sha256\": \"057fcd315c9e7f6bdd12044adacda6ebbba8f8f66c2ac397fe6dd8f45d3c8356\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \"path\": \"TURN_1_CHECKS.json\",\n+      \"bytes\": 246,\n+      \"sha256\": \"0b54d1f575bc196649f1f583a797e03e28ae707b120f08fdc00e6e02c9b13800\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \"path\": \"TURN_1_LEDGER.jsonl\",\n+      \"bytes\": 3973,\n+      \"sha256\": \"b654ca6f18208d3be7b7e54326e35e35aaf7078b4341a6976db9f85c89b4ea15\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \"path\": \"TURN_1_STATE.json\",\n+      \"bytes\": 2186,\n+      \"sha256\": \"e6f2b935143102e537866e8a2de28a36f5a2b5a43b2a3e1d9312d98fb2def739\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \"path\": \"verify_turn1.py\",\n+      \"bytes\": 1604,\n+      \"sha256\": \"d0ca73dcef35a37bb7a798b8bd6dc7c6117361690494042f4f056d9c1f5c86d7\"\n+    }\n+  ]\n+}"},{"sha":"0332ec536a31569517746e57bcf4507e868e24b4","filename":"unsolved_math_prioritization/attempts/2303016/TURN_1_STATE.json","status":"added","additions":25,"deletions":0,"changes":25,"blob_url":"https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/blob/f4039c9c093b10e651ee7fd2e6379073b84238c7/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2303016%2FTURN_1_STATE.json","raw_url":"https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/raw/f4039c9c093b10e651ee7fd2e6379073b84238c7/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2303016%2FTURN_1_STATE.json","contents_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/AlecKriebel/Math/contents/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2303016%2FTURN_1_STATE.json?ref=f4039c9c093b10e651ee7fd2e6379073b84238c7","patch":"@@ -0,0 +1,25 @@\n+{\n+  \"at\": \"2026-10-02T16:23:15+00:00\",\n+  \"id\": \"2303016\",\n+  \"status\": \"candidate_result\",\n+  \"note\": \"Turn1 complete direct Newtonian energy proof of the source thin-point conclusion, credited to classical capacitary strategy; explicit weak potential bound and Borel localization, critical-power and uncountable-polar-set sharpness. No Kellogg or Wiener regularity invocation. Full candidate frozen for independent review.\",\n+  \"evidence\": {\n+    \"review_hash\": \"d483c473c60d1dad134cc52b6eadfb1abac45bf24452902f79e17bdfaaab910c\",\n+    \"exact_claim\": \"For compact E in R^n,n>=3, give a direct proof that the points x in E where integral_0^1 cap(E intersect closed B(x,r))/r^(n-1) dr is finite form a polar set; perhaps demonstrate sharpness. Source2018 printed65/PDF66 Problem3.16, formula3.7; Update3.16 reports no progress.\",\n+    \"primary_sources\": [\n+      \"https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200\",\n+      \"https://www.numdam.org/item/10.5802/aif.944.pdf\"\n+    ],\n+    \"literature_checked_at\": \"2026-10-02T16:16:00Z\",\n+    \"success_test\": \"A direct capacity/energy argument without importing Kellogg or Dirichlet irregular-point results, with all measurability and capacity normalizations explicit. Natural sharpness claims must be precisely formulated rather than an undefined universal optimality assertion.\",\n+    \"budget\": \"Five genuine author turns unless a complete source-requested candidate earlier, then independent full review.\",\n+    \"prior_attempt_gap\": \"No exact ID/code, Kellogg, Rippon+thin PR match, nor recovered object-path/commit/recovery-inventory match. Imported report is only statement reading and web search, not proof work.\",\n+    \"duplicate_check\": \"Live branch scan and379 recovered refs; exact-ID/code/method aliases searched. A blank branch would not alone be an attempt. Inaccessible deleted objects not certified absent.\"\n+  },\n+  \"statement_hash\": \"72ddb8fb5b205e0232577f513c2371d76b4ce02dfaf6b6358f964a583920f040\",\n+  \"review_hash\": \"d483c473c60d1dad134cc52b6eadfb1abac45bf24452902f79e17bdfaaab910c\",\n+  \"turns_used\": 1,\n+  \"readiness_review_hash\": \"d483c473c60d1dad134cc52b6eadfb1abac45bf24452902f79e17bdfaaab910c\",\n+  \"event\": \"proof_attempt_turn\",\n+  \"candidate_turn\": 1\n+}"},{"sha":"c3165bf6a833b3212a3daa193404ea3678f109f3","filename":"unsolved_math_prioritization/attempts/2303016/final_review/CHECKS.json","status":"added","additions":7,"deletions":0,"changes":7,"blob_url":"https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/blob/f4039c9c093b10e651ee7fd2e6379073b84238c7/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2303016%2Ffinal_review%2FCHECKS.json","raw_url":"https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/raw/f4039c9c093b10e651ee7fd2e6379073b84238c7/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2303016%2Ffinal_review%2FCHECKS.json","contents_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/AlecKriebel/Math/contents/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2303016%2Ffinal_review%2FCHECKS.json?ref=f4039c9c093b10e651ee7fd2e6379073b84238c7","patch":"@@ -0,0 +1,7 @@\n+{\n+  \"status\": \"PASS\",\n+  \"manifest_bound_files\": 13,\n+  \"independent_assertions\": 1909,\n+  \"author_assertions\": 2690,\n+  \"scope\": \"Integrity, exact scalar bounds and variation identity. Analytical all-set proof audited separately; no finite controls certify polarity.\"\n+}"},{"sha":"54a20110e15dbe3b7a2a26dc5fc3da208c903750","filename":"unsolved_math_prioritization/attempts/2303016/final_review/REVIEW.md","status":"added","additions":27,"deletions":0,"changes":27,"blob_url":"https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/blob/f4039c9c093b10e651ee7fd2e6379073b84238c7/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2303016%2Ffinal_review%2FREVIEW.md","raw_url":"https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/raw/f4039c9c093b10e651ee7fd2e6379073b84238c7/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2303016%2Ffinal_review%2FREVIEW.md","contents_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/AlecKriebel/Math/contents/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2303016%2Ffinal_review%2FREVIEW.md?ref=f4039c9c093b10e651ee7fd2e6379073b84238c7","patch":"@@ -0,0 +1,27 @@\n+# Full independent method/proof review: 2303016\n+\n+PASS: credited direct proof, already_solved1/5. No historical novelty certification. The source asks for a direct proof, not merely the known Kellogg conclusion; the packet meets that requirement.\n+\n+## Source and method\n+\n+Hayman–Lingham printed65, Problem3.16 and its update were visually inspected. The exact closed-ball Newtonian capacity integral and n>=3 are retained. Hedberg–Wolff's Borel-set proof of Theorem2, pp173–174, was read for its capacitary-measure and small-integral contradiction strategy. That strategy is explicitly credited. The 2018 editors' 'no progress reported' sentence is not misrepresented as an exhaustive literature conclusion.\n+\n+## Analytical audit\n+\n+Every section of TURN_1 was checked. Compact finite-energy minimizers exist by weak compactness and lower semicontinuity; restrictions have finite cross energy and justify the first variation. Variation implies U>=J almost everywhere, and its energy integral gives equality. Lower semicontinuity has the correct direction: approximating support points by a dense equality set gives U<=J on support. The nearest-support distance inequality yields the global 2^s J bound without importing an equilibrium quasi-everywhere theorem. Restricted probability normalization is m², giving precisely mu(A)<=2^s J cap(A).\n+\n+The closed-ball compact intersections have upper-semicontinuous capacities under center/radius convergence: containment in slightly enlarged limiting balls and continuity on decreasing compacts prove this. Parameter integration is Borel. The finiteness set is therefore Borel, enabling ordinary Choquet capacitability. A finite-energy measure, measurable small-tail exhaustion, countable small-cube cover and inner regularity give an actual compact positive-capacity subset with the tail bound at every point. No unjustified uniform convergence on all thin points is assumed.\n+\n+Tonelli gives the layer-cake identity even on the infinite diagonal. Applying restriction domination below delta and total mass above it yields U<=J/4+delta^(-s). The diameter lower bound on J and diam(K)<=delta/4 give the second term<=J/4, because s=n-2>=1. This contradicts the measure-almost-everywhere equality U=J>0. All quantifiers and scales match the original integral.\n+\n+Borel capacitability and capacity-zero/polar equivalence remain explicit standard foundations. They are not the fine-topological Choquet property, Kellogg theorem or Wiener boundary regularity criterion. Their use is noncircular relative to the requested direct argument. This review does not claim to re-prove foundational potential theory.\n+\n+## Sharpness\n+\n+Weakening the denominator exponent by any positive amount makes the integral finite at every point by the ball-capacity upper bound; a ball has positive capacity by finite volume-measure energy. The dyadic line-segment argument is correct, including endpoints handled by a disjoint interval partition: same-bin pairs have distance at most the bin diameter and squared masses sum to at least the reciprocal number of bins. Its infinite energy makes the uncountable segment polar. These establish critical power and uncountable exceptional-set sharpness, not an unformulated universal statement for every nonintegrable gauge.\n+\n+## Computation and disposition\n+\n+All13 files bound by the frozen manifest were checked and the author's2690 scalar controls replayed byte-exact. Independent check.py performs1909 integrity/algebra/scalar assertions, including rational distances beyond the author's reciprocal-integer tests. These controls supplement the complete analytical proof and do not certify polarity by finite sampling.\n+\n+Publish as credited already_solved1/5, prominently describing the method-compliant reconstruction and limited sharpness claims. Preserve frozen author bytes. No essential gap found."},{"sha":"b63144c2dec46bec58512d8a7c2b4f75b9985847","filename":"unsolved_math_prioritization/attempts/2303016/final_review/REVIEW_MANIFEST.json","status":"added","additions":21,"deletions":0,"changes":21,"blob_url":"https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/blob/f4039c9c093b10e651ee7fd2e6379073b84238c7/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2303016%2Ffinal_review%2FREVIEW_MANIFEST.json","raw_url":"https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/raw/f4039c9c093b10e651ee7fd2e6379073b84238c7/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2303016%2Ffinal_review%2FREVIEW_MANIFEST.json","contents_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/AlecKriebel/Math/contents/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2303016%2Ffinal_review%2FREVIEW_MANIFEST.json?ref=f4039c9c093b10e651ee7fd2e6379073b84238c7","patch":"@@ -0,0 +1,21 @@\n+{\n+  \"problem_id\": 2303016,\n+  \"verdict\": \"PASS credited direct proof\",\n+  \"files\": [\n+    {\n+      \"path\": \"CHECKS.json\",\n+      \"bytes\": 267,\n+      \"sha256\": \"bf6c5c2d1975f8dc4835750f47c35e6a05647dcbed8a96a61f7a209b63ad9f75\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \"path\": \"REVIEW.md\",\n+      \"bytes\": 3860,\n+      \"sha256\": \"a6a67e1ab4818152c4f6f4c19a83f93619ec998d323f74e9b7d590e4e1b48bb4\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \"path\": \"check.py\",\n+      \"bytes\": 1410,\n+      \"sha256\": \"3fc7579b71b11a0a2a768c685ad63f60775da840903bef0ccfe8e70a849d05f3\"\n+    }\n+  ]\n+}"},{"sha":"42b723490f7b16e40ec8fb44cc27428450b3ad4a","filename":"unsolved_math_prioritization/attempts/2303016/final_review/check.py","status":"added","additions":19,"deletions":0,"changes":19,"blob_url":"https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/blob/f4039c9c093b10e651ee7fd2e6379073b84238c7/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2303016%2Ffinal_review%2Fcheck.py","raw_url":"https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/raw/f4039c9c093b10e651ee7fd2e6379073b84238c7/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2303016%2Ffinal_review%2Fcheck.py","contents_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/AlecKriebel/Math/contents/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2303016%2Ffinal_review%2Fcheck.py?ref=f4039c9c093b10e651ee7fd2e6379073b84238c7","patch":"@@ -0,0 +1,19 @@\n+from pathlib import Path\n+from fractions import Fraction as Q\n+import hashlib,json,subprocess,sys\n+p=Path(sys.argv[1]) if len(sys.argv)>1 else Path('/workspace/shared/research_thinpoints_2303016/checkpoint')\n+m=json.loads((p/'FINAL_FROZEN_MANIFEST.json').read_text());N=0\n+for f in m['files']:assert hashlib.sha256((p/f['path']).read_bytes()).hexdigest()==f['sha256'];N+=1\n+r=subprocess.check_output([sys.executable,str(p/'verify_turn1.py')]);assert r==(p/'TURN_1_CHECKS.json').read_bytes()\n+# Independently check sharp dyadic scalar envelope at arbitrary rational distances,\n+# not only reciprocal integers, and localization constants on exact rationals.\n+for a in range(1,51):\n+ for b in range(a,61):\n+  x=Q(a,b);v=sum((Q(2)**(k-1) for k in range(16) if x<=Q(1,2**k)),Q(0));assert v<=1/x;N+=1\n+for s in range(1,41):\n+ eps=Q(1,4*2**s*s)\n+ for ratio in [Q(1,4),Q(1,5),Q(1,9)]:assert 2**s*s*eps+ratio**s<=Q(1,2);N+=1\n+# First variation polynomial coefficients checked symbolically.\n+import sympy as S\n+t,J,L,H=S.symbols('t J L H');poly=S.expand((1-t)**2*J+2*t*(1-t)*L+t*t*H);assert S.diff(poly,t).subs(t,0)==2*(L-J);N+=1\n+print(json.dumps({'status':'PASS','manifest_bound_files':len(m['files']),'independent_assertions':N,'author_assertions':json.loads(r)['assertions'],'scope':'Integrity, exact scalar bounds and variation identity. Analytical all-set proof audited separately; no finite controls certify polarity.'},indent=2))"},{"sha":"a27729b4ed27d7c09ab5fc4fe49993e4d13b6a5c","filename":"unsolved_math_prioritization/attempts/2303016/verify_turn1.py","status":"added","additions":34,"deletions":0,"changes":34,"blob_url":"https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/blob/f4039c9c093b10e651ee7fd2e6379073b84238c7/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2303016%2Fverify_turn1.py","raw_url":"https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/raw/f4039c9c093b10e651ee7fd2e6379073b84238c7/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2303016%2Fverify_turn1.py","contents_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/AlecKriebel/Math/contents/unsolved_math_prioritization%2Fattempts%2F2303016%2Fverify_turn1.py?ref=f4039c9c093b10e651ee7fd2e6379073b84238c7","patch":"@@ -0,0 +1,34 @@\n+from fractions import Fraction as F\n+import json\n+checks=0\n+def check(x):\n+ global checks\n+ assert x;checks+=1\n+# Constants in the energy contradiction, for every tested ambient dimension.\n+for n in range(3,31):\n+ s=n-2;C=2**s;eps=F(1,4*C*s)\n+ check(C*s*eps==F(1,4))\n+ check(F(1,4**s)<=F(1,4));check(C*s*eps+F(1,4**s)<=F(1,2))\n+ for j in range(1,31):\n+  delta=F(1,j);D=delta/4;Jmin=D**(-s)\n+  check(delta**(-s)/Jmin==F(1,4**s))\n+ # Pointwise nearest-support estimate, expressed in scalar distance bounds.\n+ for d in [F(1,3),F(1),F(3)]:\n+  for e in [F(1,100),F(1,5)]:\n+   for b in [d,2*d,5*d]:\n+    a=d+e+b;factor=2+e/d\n+    check(a<=factor*b);check(b**(-s)<=factor**s*a**(-s))\n+ # Power-weakened integral upper bound has positive finite exponent.\n+ for eta in [F(1,10),F(1,2),F(1),F(2)]:check(eta>0 and F(C)/eta>0)\n+# Exact quadratic energy variation identity and its first-order coefficient.\n+for J in [F(1),F(2),F(5)]:\n+ for L in [F(1),F(3),F(7)]:\n+  for N in [F(2),F(4),F(9)]:\n+   for t in [F(0),F(1,10),F(1,2),F(1)]:\n+    check((1-t)**2*J+2*t*(1-t)*L+t*t*N==J+2*(L-J)*t+(J-2*L+N)*t*t)\n+# Dyadic kernel lower bound and mass Cauchy lower bound.\n+for j in range(1,513):\n+ d=F(1,j);partial=sum((F(2)**(k-1) for k in range(13) if d<=F(1,2**k)),F(0));check(partial<=1/d)\n+for k in range(13):\n+ N=2**k;check(N*F(1,N)**2==F(1,N));check(F(2)**(k-1)*F(1,N)==F(1,2))\n+print(json.dumps(dict(assertions=checks,scope='Exact constants, distance inequalities, first-variation algebra and dyadic sharpness sanity checks; the all-set proof is TURN_1.md.',method='No finite computation certifies polarity or capacitability.'),indent=2,sort_keys=True))"}]]